article_page('dowry-daughter-rights-partition-suits', <<<'HTML'
<p>Daughters now have equal rights in ancestral property. Yet in partition disputes, brothers sometimes argue that a sister has "already received her share" through the dowry, jewellery or property given at her marriage. Does that argument hold up?</p>
<h2>Daughters' equal rights since 2005</h2>
<p>The Hindu Succession Act, 1956 governs inheritance among Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and Sikhs. Historically, daughters were not treated as equal heirs in ancestral property. The <strong>Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005</strong> changed that: daughters became coparceners by birth, with the same rights and liabilities as sons.</p>
<h2>What happens to property given at marriage?</h2>
<p>Giving dowry is prohibited under the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961, but in practice many families still give gifts, jewellery, money or property at a daughter's marriage. Legally, gifts given to a woman at or around her marriage generally form her <strong>stridhan</strong>, her absolute property.</p>
<p>When a daughter later files a partition suit, the question is whether those gifts should be taken into account. Gifts given at marriage do not, by themselves, take away a daughter's statutory right as a coparcener. Whether anything given to her affects the division depends on the facts, for example whether property was formally transferred to her as part of a partition or a family settlement, and what documents exist.</p>
<h2>Key considerations in a partition suit</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nature of the property:</strong> is it ancestral (coparcenary), self-acquired by a parent, or a gift?</li>
<li><strong>Earlier partitions or settlements:</strong> was there a registered partition or family settlement in which she received a share?</li>
<li><strong>Documents:</strong> deeds, settlement papers and records showing what was given and in what capacity.</li>
<li><strong>Stridhan:</strong> property given to her as a gift belongs to her and is separate from the family estate.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>How gifts made at a daughter's marriage interact with her share in a partition is a nuanced question that needs careful examination of the facts and documents. What is clear is that the 2005 amendment gives daughters equal coparcenary rights, and courts look closely to make sure those rights are not defeated by informal arrangements.</p>
